        real(8), parameter :: bequests_to_net_wealth_data=0.012
        ! AN ANNUAL RATE, NOT A POINT-IN-TIME ONE, from 6 September. The author:
        ! *"Ok, so we can match 1-1.2%... Let's see if we can do that instead!"*
        !
        ! It was 0.00399: HUD point in time 2023 converted to HOUSEHOLDS, 467,020
        ! homeless individuals at one household each plus 57,563 homeless family
        ! households, against 131,332,360 occupied units. The model's agent is a
        ! household, so the person rate is the wrong denominator; before that it
        ! was 0.0045.
        !
        ! WHY THE POINT-IN-TIME FIGURE IS THE WRONG OBJECT. One model period is a
        ! YEAR. A household that spends any part of a period on the street is
        ! homeless for the whole of it, so model_fraction_homeless is "homeless at
        ! some point during the year" and HUD's January single-night count is not.
        ! The two differ by the ratio of annual prevalence to point prevalence,
        ! which is above one for any positive exit rate and large here because
        ! spells are short.
        !
        ! THE MULTIPLIER IS PROVISIONAL AND SAYS SO. HUD's AHAR Part 2 reports
        ! annual SHELTERED homelessness at roughly twice the point-in-time
        ! sheltered count, and the shelter-spell literature puts the all-in annual
        ! figure near two and a half to three times point prevalence, since the
        ! unsheltered turn over faster. 2.75 is the middle of that and 0.00399 x
        ! 2.75 = 0.01097.
        !
        ! THIS NUMBER MUST BE REBUILT FROM AHAR PART 2 THE WAY 0.00399 WAS BUILT
        ! FROM THE PIT TABLES -- annual homeless HOUSEHOLDS over occupied units,
        ! not a multiplier applied to a household rate. Until then it is a
        ! defensible order of magnitude and not a citable figure, and the paper
        ! must not quote it as one.
        !
        ! 0.005 FROM 7 September, the author's choice: *"let's set h1 to target
        ! 0.5%"*. It sits between HUD's point-in-time household rate of 0.00399,
        ! which is measured but is the wrong object for an annual period, and the
        ! 0.01097 above, which is the right object but is 0.00399 times a
        ! multiplier of 2.75 that this comment already refuses to let the paper
        ! quote. The model was running at 0.0046 to 0.0048 when they set it.
        !
        ! IT IS STILL NOT A CITABLE FIGURE and the rebuild above is still owed.
        ! Nothing here changes that; 0.005 is a working target, and the AHAR Part
        ! 2 construction is what the paper will have to quote.
        real(8), parameter :: fraction_homeless_data=0.005

        ! THE DISTRIBUTION OF HOUSEHOLDS ACROSS UNIT SIZES, from the American
        ! Community Survey 2023 table B25042, tenure by bedrooms, universe
        ! OCCUPIED housing units, N = 131,332,360. Built by
        ! data/make_housing_dist.py into data/us_housing_dist.csv, with the six
        ! bedroom categories collapsed to three: no bedroom and one bedroom, two
        ! bedrooms, and three or more.
        !
        ! WHY THIS RATHER THAN THE HOUSING COST SHARES. The two rental sizes used
        ! to be identified from the share of renters spending above 30 or above
        ! 50 per cent of income on housing. That is identification from a budget
        ! CONSEQUENCE of the grid rather than from the grid itself, so the sizes
        ! absorbed anything else that moved the housing cost distribution:
        ! incomes, rents, the voucher, the food stamp floor. The size
        ! distribution names the object directly. The cost shares stay in the
        ! table as untargeted moments, where they are a check rather than a
        ! definition.
        !
        ! The universe is occupied units, so the model counterpart conditions on
        ! being HOUSED: the homeless are not in the ACS denominator and are not
        ! in this one either.
        real(8), parameter, dimension(0:2) :: housing_size_dist_data=&
            (/0.132065d0,0.241212d0,0.626723d0/)

        ! H_2 IS THE SEVERE COST BURDEN NOW, NOT THE SMALLEST UNIT'S SHARE.
        !
        ! The author's instruction on 30 August. The smallest unit's share is a
        ! statement about a grid the model chose, so matching it disciplines the
        ! grid rather than the economy; the share of households paying more than
        ! half their income for shelter is a fact about the world, and it is what
        ! the bottom of the ladder ought to be identified on.
        !
        ! 0.101 is SCF 2022, ALL households, owners and renters together, housing
        ! cost above half of income. Owner cost is SCF's pirmort, twelve times the
        ! monthly mortgage payment over income, scaled by 1.5 to stand in for
        ! property tax and utilities -- the summary extract carries neither, so
        ! that factor is an assumption and not a measurement. Unscaled the figure
        ! is 0.078 and at 1.3 it is 0.092, so the assumption is worth about two
        ! points. Renters alone give 0.181 and owners alone 0.025.
        !
        ! ALL households is the right universe BECAUSE EVERYONE RENTS HERE:
        ! include_homeownership is 0, so the model's renters stand for the whole
        ! population rather than for the third of it that rents in the data.
        ! THE DENOMINATOR MISMATCH IS KNOWN AND THE AUTHOR HAS ACCEPTED IT.
        ! Do not "fix" this by moving the target or the object.
        !
        ! The model object above is val_burden50_expend_all over
        ! val_mass_expend_all -- the headcount above half of total SPENDING. This
        ! constant is SCF 2022, above half of INCOME. They are not the same
        ! denominator, and on 1 September the mismatch was put to the author
        ! explicitly, alongside the two consistent alternatives: point at rent50
        ! against the 0.181 recorded above for SCF renters alone, or compute a CEX
        ! headcount over total outlays to match the 26.8 gamma uses. Their answer
        ! was "YES, USE the 10.1% ... i think that works fine."
        !
        ! So it stands, and the reason it is defensible is that income and
        ! expenditure differ by saving, which is small in the tail this headcount
        ! counts: a household paying more than half of income for shelter is not
        ! saving much, so its income and its outlays are close and the two
        ! headcounts nearly coincide there. The bias is toward the model reading
        ! LOW against this target, since expenditure exceeds income for the
        ! dissaving poor.
        !
        ! WHAT THE PAPER OWES THE READER: the calibration table should say the
        ! model counterpart is measured over total expenditure while the SCF
        ! figure is over income, rather than letting the row imply they match.
        real(8), parameter :: severe_cost_burden_data=0.101d0
        real(8), parameter :: fraction_light_users=0.731
        
