        ! RETARGETED TO 0.139 ON THE AUTHOR'S INSTRUCTION, 1 September.
        !
        ! It was 0.0485, the U-3 unemployment rate, which is a PERSON level rate
        ! with the labour force as its denominator. This is a HOUSEHOLD model and
        ! model_fraction_unemployed counts every working-age household with
        ! emp_of /= 1 -- that is emp_of 3 on UI and emp_of 2 with nothing --
        ! over all working-age households. Those are different numerators and
        ! different denominators, and one state space cannot satisfy both.
        !
        ! The comparable figure is the one already printed beside
        ! "DIAG households with no wage income, working age": SCF 2022 puts 13.9
        ! per cent of households aged 26 to 64 with NO wage income. That share
        ! already contains the unemployed, so it is 0.139 and not 0.139 plus a
        ! separate unemployment rate; adding them would count the same households
        ! twice.
        !
        ! What this buys: at 0.0485 the model had only about five per cent of
        ! working-age households without wage income to draw the homeless from,
        ! against 54 per cent of the homeless being out of work in the data. The
        ! note at probability_of_ui says the same thing from the other side --
        ! "homelessness could only ever be drawn from the 5.7 per cent
        ! temporarily unemployed on UI, never from households with nothing".
        !
        ! What it does NOT buy, and this is measured rather than assumed:
        ! joblessness is currently PROTECTIVE here, the homelessness rate among
        ! the unemployed being 0.43 times the working-age rate. Enlarging the
        ! pool does not by itself put anyone on the street.
        ! 4.9 PER CENT, on the author's instruction of 2 September. It was
        ! 0.139, which is the SCF 2022 share of households aged 26-64 with NO
        ! WAGE INCOME -- the figure the diagnostic at 5969 is labelled with --
        ! and the author's reason for it then was "those are people not
        ! working". 4.9 is the conventional unemployment rate.
        !
        ! WORTH CHECKING BEFORE THIS IS RELIED ON, and not checked here: whether
        ! model_fraction_unemployed is the same OBJECT as a published
        ! unemployment rate. It is a share of the model's population in the
        ! unemployed state, and the model has no separate participation margin
        ! for it, so it may be nearer the no-wage-income share than the BLS rate.
        ! If so the two figures are both right about different things and the
        ! model's counterpart is the 13.9. The moment stays scored either way;
        ! this note is about which number it should be scored against.
        ! BACK TO 0.139 ON THE AUTHOR'S INSTRUCTION, 3 September: *"I think maybe
        ! we need to have more unemployment. Can we revert to all those
        ! unemployed and not in the labor force? We had 13.9% I think. That is
        ! better than 4.9%"*.
        !
        ! The note above already said this and flagged it as unchecked: "whether
        ! model_fraction_unemployed is the same OBJECT as a published
        ! unemployment rate... it may be nearer the no-wage-income share than the
        ! BLS rate. If so the two figures are both right about different things
        ! and the model's counterpart is the 13.9." It is checked now, and it is.
        ! model_fraction_unemployed counts every working-age household with
        ! emp_of /= 1 -- on UI, or with nothing -- over all working-age
        ! households. That is a HOUSEHOLD share with no participation margin, so
        ! its data counterpart is the SCF 2022 share of households aged 26 to 64
        ! with NO WAGE INCOME, 13.9 per cent, not the U-3 person rate of 4.9.
        !
        ! IT IS REPORT-ONLY FOR A SINGLE POINT. The one other use of this
        ! constant is the separation-rate adjuster in the outer calibration loop,
        ! which run_one_point.sh does not run: pi_eu sits at 0.09185 with the
        ! handed-in probability_becoming_unemployed of 0.0122, unadjusted. So no
        ! equilibrium object moves and the archive pools across the change.
        !
        ! The gap goes from +87.5 per cent against 0.049 to -33.9 against 0.139.
        ! RETARGETED TO 0.049 ON THE AUTHOR'S INSTRUCTION, 4 September, after the
        ! saturation diagnosis below. Their words: *"maybe we should stay with an
        ! unemployment rate of 4.9% then, it seems to be a better fit"*, and *"it
        ! would allow more reasonable numbers"*.
        !
        ! THE OBJECTION WAS PUT AND THE AUTHOR REAFFIRMED, so it is recorded here
        ! rather than argued again. The model has NO LABOUR FORCE PARTICIPATION
        ! MARGIN -- emp_of is 1 employed, 2 unemployed with nothing, 3 on UI, and
        ! there is no out-of-the-labour-force state -- so unemployed over
        ! (employed + unemployed) IS what this moment already computes. In the data
        ! roughly 38 per cent of the working age population is out of the labour
        ! force and the model counts all of them as unemployed, which is why the
        ! honest counterpart was the SCF household no-wage-income share of 13.9.
        ! At 4.9 the model will put a wage earner in about 95 per cent of
        ! working-age households against 86 in the data, so household joblessness
        ! is understated roughly threefold.
        !
        ! WHAT IT BUYS, and it is not small. At 0.139 the only instrument was a
        ! separation rate that had to reach 0.055, where min(pbu*kappa_uem, 1)
        ! puts EVERY mentally ill worker at certain unemployment and employment
        ! given SMI collapses to 1.1 per cent against its own 30 per cent target --
        ! a moment that is computed and NOT scored, so nothing would have caught
        ! it. At 0.049 the separation rate falls to about 0.0032, nothing
        ! saturates, and the SMI employment margin becomes economics again.
        ! 0.049 -> 0.1393, ON THE AUTHOR'S INSTRUCTION of 5 September: *"maybe we
        ! need to bring the unemployment rate back up to 13.9%?"*. This RESTORES the
        ! earlier target and the block above explains why it was abandoned; that
        ! reason no longer holds.
        !
        ! pi_eu IS THE HOUSEHOLD NO-WAGE-INCOME SHARE, not the labour force
        ! unemployment rate: the model prints "households with no wage income,
        ! working age" and it is the SAME NUMBER as the pi_eu moment, to every
        ! digit. So 0.1393, the SCF share of working-age households with no wage
        ! earner, is the concept this moment actually measures and 0.049 was the
        ! wrong quantity attached to the right diagnostic.
        !
        ! WHY IT WAS BACKED OFF, AND WHY THAT IS FIXED. At 0.1393 the separation
        ! rate must reach about 0.055, where min(pbu*kappa_uem,1) saturates and puts
        ! EVERY mentally ill worker at certain unemployment: employment given SMI
        ! collapses to 1.1 per cent against its 30 per cent target. The note above
        ! says the danger was that this is *"a moment that is computed and NOT
        ! scored, so nothing would have caught it"*.
        !
        ! kappa_uem IS SCORED NOW, reconciled into next_batch.py's loss on
        ! 5 September, and it owns its own moment with a rule that LOWERS the
        ! parameter when SMI employment falls below target. The saturation can no
        ! longer happen silently: it is one of the nineteen gaps the walk reads
        ! every iteration.
        ! 0.1393 -> 0.049, ON THE AUTHOR'S INSTRUCTION of 6 September: *"Maybe
        ! what we need is a lower unemployment rate? The 4.9%...would that help?"*
        ! and, when the concern below was put to them, *"Let the method run with
        ! the new unemployment rate target"*.
        !
        ! THE ARITHMETIC THAT MOTIVATES IT IS SOUND. At the 0.268 housing-weight
        ! point the model has 14.06 per cent of working-age households with no
        ! wage income and 29.51 per cent of THEM homeless, and 0.1406 x 0.2951 =
        ! 0.0415 against a measured h_1 of 0.0582. Cutting the pool to 4.9 per
        ! cent at the same conditional rate gives 0.0145 against the annual target
        ! of 0.01097 -- from 5.3 times out to 1.3. It works through the population
        ! at risk rather than the price of the street, so unlike every other lever
        ! measured on 6 September it does not drag emp_homeless down with it.
        !
        ! THE CONCERN, RECORDED BECAUSE IT WAS RAISED AND OVERRULED. This moment
        ! is the HOUSEHOLD NO-WAGE-INCOME SHARE, not the labour-force unemployment
        ! rate: the model prints "households with no wage income, working age" and
        ! it equals the pi_eu moment to five digits. 0.1393 is the SCF share of
        ! working-age households with no wage earner; 0.049 is the BLS
        ! labour-force rate, a different denominator and a different universe that
        ! excludes everyone out of the labour force. Those households are the ones
        ! this model puts at risk of homelessness. The author moved the target the
        ! OTHER way on 5 September for exactly this reason, and the comment block
        ! that used to sit here said 0.049 was "the wrong quantity attached to the
        ! right diagnostic".
        !
        ! WHAT WOULD ANSWER THE CONCERN WITHOUT MOVING THE TARGET is the
        ! CONDITIONAL rate: 29.51 per cent of no-wage households homeless is the
        ! indefensible number, not the size of the pool. Eight per cent there
        ! gives h_1 = 0.011 with the pool untouched. replacement_rate_ui was made
        ! calibratable on 6 September precisely to attack it and has not been
        ! swept from a good base.
        real(8), parameter :: unemployment_rate_data=0.049
        real(8), parameter :: unemployment_rate_sud_data=0.1625

        real(8) :: relative_share_of_addiction_adj=5*10.d0**(-2)
        real(8) :: distance_relative_share_of_addiction_1
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_relative_share_of_addiction_1=10.d0**(-3)

        real(8) :: addiction_scalar_adjuster=2*10.d0**(-1)
        real(8) :: distance_addiction_scalar
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_addiction_scalar=10.d0**(-3)

        ! B OSCILLATES WITHOUT DAMPING, because participation is far more elastic
        ! in it than a fixed adjuster assumes. Measured: b rose 48.7 per cent and
        ! participation fell from 0.176 to 0.0136, past a target of 0.0513 and by
        ! a factor of thirteen. An undamped rule then reverses and overshoots the
        ! other way for ever, and it takes the SUD rate with it, because the
        ! households who convert are drawn from the ones who use.
        !
        ! Halving the step whenever the gap changes sign turns the rule into a
        ! bisection, which converges on any monotone response however steep.
        ! Growing it back slowly when the sign holds keeps it from creeping once
        ! the oscillation has been squeezed out. Same idiom as delta_d's.
        real(8), parameter :: addiction_scalar_adjuster_start=2*10.d0**(-1)
        real(8), parameter :: addiction_scalar_damping=0.5
        real(8), parameter :: addiction_scalar_growth=1.2
        real(8), parameter :: addiction_scalar_floor=10.d0**(-4)
        ! The band theta and b are held in. See the two update rules for why
        ! they are absolute rather than only trust regions.
        ! THE CEILING IS SET BY THE BREAK POINT, not by taste. The stylized
        ! model's appendix scan finds that by theta = 0.23 the mortality channel
        ! no longer reverses the income gradient and the average user stops
        ! being poorer than average: a drug that valuable is not deterred by a
        ! death that arrives later. Above that point the model cannot produce
        ! the fact it exists to explain, whatever the death penalty is set to,
        ! which is why sweeping kappa_phi_d at theta = 0.667 moved nothing.
        !
        ! 0.25 rather than 0.23, because the break was measured in the two
        ! period model and this one is annual over sixty years, so its own break
        ! need not sit in the same place. A calibration that wants to go above
        ! 0.25 is then reporting that it cannot hit the spending share without
        ! losing the gradient, which is worth seeing rather than hiding.
        real(8), parameter :: theta_lo=0.05d0
        ! RAISED TO 0.30 ON THE AUTHOR'S INSTRUCTION. The reasoning above still
        ! stands and the guard that enforces it is unchanged: theta may not rise
        ! while the income gradient is inverted, so the extra slack is available
        ! only where the model still puts the users at the bottom.
        ! RAISED AGAIN, TO 0.5, ON THE AUTHOR'S INSTRUCTION. The guard above is
        ! unchanged: theta may not rise while the income gradient is inverted,
        ! so the extra slack is available only where the model still puts the
        ! users at the bottom. The ceiling also has to clear theta's OWN
        ! declared value, which is 0.4265: at 0.30 the parameter started above
        ! the highest value the calibration was allowed to give it and the
        ! clamp pulled it down on the first read, so the declared value was
        ! never the starting point it was written to be.
        ! 0.7 ON THE AUTHOR'S INSTRUCTION, AND SO 0.55 IS A VALUE NOT A CLAMP. The handover clamps to this
        ! ceiling, so asking for 0.55 with the ceiling at 0.50 would have set
        ! 0.50 and reported success: the same silent bind that put theta at
        ! 0.29357 for a whole calibration when the ceiling was 0.30. The
        ! headroom is deliberate -- a ceiling exactly at the value being tried
        ! cannot tell a converged parameter from a pinned one.
        real(8), parameter :: theta_hi=0.70d0

        ! GAMMA IS TIED TO THETA FOR NOW, on the author's instruction:
        ! gamma = 0.28*(1-theta), so housing takes 28 per cent of whatever the
        ! drug does not. The two are exponents in the same Cobb Douglas bundle,
        ! so calibrating them independently lets the housing share drift as
        ! theta moves and the pair stop summing to anything meaningful. At
        ! theta = 0.38 this is 0.1736, and the simulation should return about 27
        ! per cent of income spent on housing.
        !
        ! It is a TIE and not a reparameterisation: gamma's own bracket is
        ! skipped while tie_gamma_to_theta is one, and the author will free it
        ! again. apply_gamma_tie is called wherever theta can change -- after
        ! the state is read, after a handover and after theta's own update --
        ! rather than once at setup, because theta moves in all three places.
        ! FREED, ON THE AUTHOR'S INSTRUCTION. gamma was the one targeted moment
        ! with no instrument: this parameter was pinned at 0.28*(1-theta), so
        ! nothing could move housing's share of expenditure.
        integer, parameter :: tie_gamma_to_theta=0
        real(8), parameter :: gamma_share_of_non_drug=0.28d0
        ! B'S CEILING HAD NO ARGUMENT BEHIND IT AND IT BOUND. At 0.05 the
        ! bracket sat exactly on it: participation came out at 76.0 per cent
        ! against a target of 8.90, the update asked for b to rise by a factor of
        ! 2.5, and the ceiling refused every time. Nothing else in the
        ! calibration can be read while that is true, because with three quarters
        ! of the population using, the users ARE the population: the average
        ! income of a user came out at 0.82 of the mean and of a household with
        ! the disorder at 1.007, which is not a finding about the income gradient
        ! but an arithmetic consequence of the extensive margin being wrong.
        !
        ! ONE RATHER THAN A GUESS AT THE RIGHT VALUE. theta's ceiling is set by an
        ! economic break point, the value above which mortality stops reversing
        ! the gradient, and it should bind when the model cannot do its job. b's
        ! should only bind where the model stops making sense, and that is where
        ! the drug is worth so little at the first unit that nobody uses at any
        ! income: the marginal value of the first unit is theta/b, so at b = 1 and
        ! theta in [0.05, 0.25] it is 0.05 to 0.25, of the same order as the
        ! marginal utility of consumption. Above that there is no interior
        ! participation to find. Where inside the band participation is matched is
        ! for the bracket to discover, not for this line to decide.
        ! THE FLOOR WAS 1e-4 AND b HAS TO GO WELL BELOW IT. b is the Stone-Geary
        ! endowment, so the marginal value of the first unit is theta/b: a
        ! SMALLER b makes starting to use more attractive, not less. With the SUD
        ! mortality multiplier flat at 8 and on the hazard, nobody in the model
        ! uses at any age, and b is the parameter that decides whether anyone
        ! uses at all. It cannot restore participation from a floor four orders
        ! of magnitude above where it needs to sit; the update's own comment puts
        ! b at order 1e-8, which is why its step is multiplicative.
        ! THE FLOOR IS 10^-10 AND NOT 10^-8, so that it cannot bind at the value
        ! the author expects b to take. The marginal value of the first unit is
        ! theta/b, so participation FALLS in b and the floor is the END OF THE
        ! SCALE WITH THE MOST USE ON IT: a floor sitting inside the plausible
        ! range refuses exactly the moves the calibration needs and reports
        ! nothing, which is the failure the ceiling had at 0.05 and which is
        ! recorded a few lines below. 10^-8 was inside that range, being the
        ! value b was expected to need; 10^-10 is two decades clear of it.
        ! THE AUTHOR LOWERED THIS ON 29 AUGUST: b may be as small as it likes so
        ! long as it is strictly positive. It was 1e-10. It has never bound --
        ! the search sits near 0.017 and b's elasticity on the drug user share
        ! is about minus fifteen, so 1e-8 would put essentially every household
        ! on drugs -- but a floor that cannot bind costs nothing and a floor
        ! that silently binds costs a mislabelled point, so it goes down.
        real(8), parameter :: b_lo=1.d0*10.d0**(-30)
        real(8), parameter :: b_hi=1.0d0

        real(8) :: gap_addiction_scalar_1=0,previous_gap_addiction_scalar=0
        integer :: have_gap_addiction_scalar=0
        real(8) :: lambda_secant_slope_1=0

        ! LAMBDA'S CLAMP IS A TRUST REGION AND HAS TO SHRINK LIKE ONE. The secant
        ! is clipped to a factor of four either way, which stops a bad step but
        ! does not stop a ringing one: the SUD rate went 0.0513 over target,
        ! 0.0112 under, then 0.1824 over, five times the target, because each
        ! secant step lands on the clamp and reverses. Halving the factor on a
        ! sign flip makes it a bisection in the same way aleph's radius is, and
        ! the floor keeps it a real step rather than a stall.
        real(8) :: lambda_clamp=1.01
        real(8), parameter :: lambda_clamp_start=1.01
        real(8), parameter :: lambda_clamp_floor=1.001
        real(8) :: gap_lambda_addiction_1=0,previous_gap_lambda_addiction=0
        integer :: have_gap_lambda_addiction=0

        real(8) :: value_of_life_adjuster=1*10.d0**(1)

        ! ALEPH'S TRUST REGION HAS TO SHRINK OR IT IS A TWO CYCLE. The adjuster
        ! is ten and the gap is tens, so the step is hundreds and ALWAYS lands on
        ! the clamp. A fixed clamp then means the rule is not "step by the gap",
        ! it is "multiply by 1.5 or by 0.5", and the sign alternates every loop
        ! because each move overshoots. Measured over five loops: 16.5, 24.75,
        ! 12.375, 18.5625, 9.28125 — ratios of exactly 1.5, 0.5, 1.5, 0.5, with a
        ! net drift down of a quarter every two loops toward the floor.
        !
        ! It costs more than aleph. size_homelessness discards its bracket
        ! whenever the value of life moves more than
        ! bracket_value_of_life_tolerance, because endpoints priced at different
        ! values of life are not a bracket, and aleph moving fifty per cent every
        ! loop discards it every loop. Homelessness slammed 0.041, 0, 0.0798 with
        ! no bracket ever surviving to bisect on.
        !
        ! Halving the RADIUS on a sign flip turns the same rule into a
        ! multiplicative bisection: 1.5 and 0.5 become 1.25 and 0.8, then 1.125
        ! and 0.889, and aleph pins down geometrically instead of ringing.
        ! Growing it back when the sign holds keeps it from creeping once the
        ! ringing has stopped. Same idiom as b's and delta_d's.
        real(8) :: value_of_life_trust=0.5
        real(8), parameter :: value_of_life_trust_start=0.5
        real(8), parameter :: value_of_life_trust_damping=0.5
        real(8), parameter :: value_of_life_trust_growth=1.2
        real(8), parameter :: value_of_life_trust_floor=10.d0**(-3)
        real(8) :: gap_value_of_life_1=0,previous_gap_value_of_life=0
        integer :: have_gap_value_of_life=0
        real(8) :: distance_value_of_life
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_value_of_life=10.d0**(-2)

