        ! INSTRUCTION. A household is born patient or impatient and never
        ! switches, so the two types share prices and the government budget and
        ! nothing else.
        !
        ! Neither is assigned. The author set both brackets: the PATIENT one on
        ! wealth over output inside its 3.0-4.5 band, which is what
        ! discount_factor was already calibrated on, and the IMPATIENT one on
        ! the wealth share of the bottom half, target 0.025, which the model
        ! currently misses at 0.131 -- more than five times too fat, and the
        ! moment that most wants this change.
        ! A RUNTIME COORDINATE, NOT A COMPILE-TIME PARAMETER, defaulting to TWO
        ! types since the author moved to the two-type model. The two-pass path fails its own equal-beta test -- with both
        ! discount factors equal it does not reproduce the one-pass economy, and
        ! the disagreement reaches the internal adjusters, so the tax rate and
        ! the separation rate converge somewhere else and every moment moves
        ! with them. Until that is repaired the one-type economy is the one that
        ! can be calibrated, and it has to be reachable without editing and
        ! rebuilding the source, which would fork the archive across two model
        ! hashes for what is properly one coordinate of a point.
        !
        ! Its only other use is the loop bound in the benchmark driver, so
        ! nothing dimensions on it and a variable is safe here.
        integer :: number_of_beta_types=2
        real(8), parameter :: beta_type_weight=0.5d0
        ! Which type is being solved and measured right now. Read by the value
        ! function recursion in place of a single discount factor.
        integer :: current_beta_type=1
        ! Set to one for every pass after the first, so the moment accumulators
        ! sum across types instead of being reset. Every ratio in the moment
        ! block is formed from accumulated components, so forming it again on
        ! the last pass gives the mixture rather than the last type.
        integer :: accumulating_beta_types=0

        ! HOW MANY PASSES HAVE ACCUMULATED INTO THE STATISTICS, which is what a
        ! recomputed mass must be scaled by. Inside the two-pass loop it is the
        ! pass number; AFTER the loop it is the total, because by then every
        ! pass has contributed. Using (accumulating_beta_types+1) was correct
        ! only inside the loop, and silently became 1 for the routines that run
        ! after it -- which is a whole second copy of the bug it was fixing.
        integer :: mass_passes=1

        ! THE POOLED MASS, ACCUMULATED, BECAUSE THE TYPES DO NOT CARRY EQUAL MASS.
        !
        ! Every chain routine recomputes the mass from distribution_stationary,
        ! which holds only the type just solved, and then scaled it by
        ! mass_passes. That is exact if and only if the discount-factor types
        ! carry the same mass. They do not. Newborn cohorts are equal, but
        ! survival is not: the impatient type is homeless and addicted more and
        ! both carry excess mortality, so it dies faster and ends the life cycle
        ! lighter.
        !
        ! The evidence is the INVARIANTS block, which is what caught this. The
        ! two histogram-mass checks, which must read exactly 1.0, read 1.19002,
        ! 1.18647 and 1.18457 at three points differing only in
        ! addiction_scalar_1. A miscount of types would be a constant -- 2.0 or
        ! 0.5 -- and would not drift with a drug parameter. A survival gap
        ! between the types is exactly what drifts with it.
        !
        ! So the mass is accumulated here, one pass at a time, instead of being
        ! reconstructed from a pass count. At one type this is identical to the
        ! old code (one pass, factor one); at two equal-mass types it is
        ! identical again; it differs only where the old code was wrong.
        !
        ! It is also correct OUTSIDE the loop, which the old code was not:
        ! mass_passes is left at number_of_beta_types when the loop ends, so a
        ! later call to a chain routine -- the omega counterfactual in
        ! print_all_moments does exactly this, with one type solved -- doubled a
        ! one-type mass. accumulating_beta_types is zero there, so these reset
        ! and hold the one type actually present.
        real(8) :: pooled_mass_all=0.d0
        real(8) :: pooled_mass_retirees=0.d0
        real(8) :: pooled_mass_youngest=0.d0
        ! Each type's own mass, kept so the ratio between them can be printed
