        ! THE MOST ANY PARAMETER MAY MOVE IN ONE LOOP, on the author's
        ! instruction, and ONE constant so it is changed in one place.
        !
        ! Measured over a single loop, with the report finally pairing each
        ! parameter with the moment it produced, three instruments moved a
        ! hundred per cent and landed on their floor and a fourth moved
        ! seventy five, while every other one moved under eight. A parameter
        ! that lands on its bound every loop is not being calibrated, and the
        ! moments measured beside it are measured at the bound.
        !
        ! WHAT THIS COSTS, because the number is a judgement and the arithmetic
        ! should be visible when it is changed. A cap of one per cent closes a
        ! gap of forty per cent in about thirty five loops and one of eighty in
        ! about sixty; at roughly twenty minutes a loop that is twelve to twenty
        ! hours. A cap of five per cent closes the same gaps in seven and twelve
        ! loops, and on the measured data it would still bind on nothing except
        ! the four that were slamming, since the healthy instruments move
        ! between nought and seven per cent unaided.
        !
        ! b IS DELIBERATELY NOT CAPPED BY THIS. Its bracket spans six decades --
        ! 10^-7 gives participation of 0.748 and 0.0827 gives zero -- and at one
        ! per cent a loop it could not cross a single decade in five hundred
        ! loops. It is bounded to a factor of ten a loop and, while the sweep is
        ! running, is not moved by the loop at all.
        real(8), parameter :: max_move_per_loop=1.d0*10.d0**(-2)
        real(8), parameter :: trust_factor=1.d0+max_move_per_loop

        ! ESCAPE AND REFINE ARE DIFFERENT PROBLEMS AND NEED DIFFERENT STEPS.
        !
        ! One per cent a loop is the right step when the model is near its
        ! target and wrong when it is in a corner, because a corner is not a
        ! small distance to walk: a participation rate of 0.905 against 0.089,
        ! or homelessness of exactly zero, is the absence of the margin the
        ! parameter acts on, and refining toward it a hundredth at a time
        ! spends a day discovering that. The two regimes are told apart by the
        ! MOMENT, not by the parameter: a moment below a tenth of its target or
        ! above five times it is a corner, and the parameter that owns it is
        ! allowed to move fast until the moment is back in range. Everything
        ! else keeps the one per cent.
        real(8), parameter :: corner_low=0.1d0
        real(8), parameter :: corner_high=5.d0
        real(8), parameter :: escape_factor=2.d0
        real(8), parameter :: escape_factor_b=10.d0
        ! THE STOPPING RULE EXISTED AND THE DISTANCE DID NOT.
        !
        ! The retention scale is updated every loop against an average labour
        ! income tax rate of 22.4 per cent, and this tolerance was declared for
        ! it, but no distance was ever computed and nothing was ever tested. So
        ! the sweep could declare itself converged with the average tax rate
        ! anywhere at all, which makes every fiscal number downstream of it,
        ! including the cost of either programme, unanchored. The note says this
        ! parameter reproduces 22.4 per cent; now it has to.
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_labor_income_tax_rate=10.d0**(-3)
        real(8) :: distance_labor_income_tax_rate=1
                
        real(8) :: curvature_of_labor_income_taxes=0.85

        ! tau_k is investment_income_tax, declared near the voucher block and set
        ! at 0.15, which is the note's value. It is applied in
        ! calculate_after_tax_wealth and its revenue is accumulated in
        ! govt_revenues_from_investment_income. I reported capital income as
        ! untaxed on the strength of grepping for tau_k, which is the note's
        ! name for it and not the code's. It was there all along.
        
        ! H_3 IS EXTERNAL NOW, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES THE LADDER IDENTIFIED.
        !
        ! Three rungs were carrying three moments: h_2 on the employed share of
        ! A NAMING COLLISION THE RENAME CREATED, AND IT WILL BITE SOMEONE.
        ! The comments in this block use h_2, h_3 and h_4 for the three rental
        ! BINS of the grid, smallest, middle and largest, and that is what the
        ! algebra below means by them. The MOMENTS printed for the search now use
        ! h_2 for the severe cost burden and h_3 for the share in the LARGEST
        ! bin, on the author's instruction of 30 August. So h_3 in this block is
        ! the middle bin and h_3 in the output is the top one. They are different
        ! objects with the same name; read the block, not the label.
        !
        ! the homeless, varsigma on the middle bin's share and h_4 on the top
        ! bin's. Three shares carry only two free numbers, so the third was
        ! always going to be absorbed somewhere, and it was absorbed by the
        ! weight on housing: gamma came out at 0.088 against a housing
        ! expenditure share of 0.35, which is a weight doing no work because the
        ! quantities are doing it instead.
        !
        ! So the middle rung is FIXED, at the midpoint between h_2 and h_4, and
        ! varsigma is no longer calibrated. h_2 is set on the employed share of
        ! the homeless and h_4 on the top bin's mass, one moment each, and the
        ! middle bin's share falls out of the model as a check rather than being
        ! aimed at. At varsigma = 1 the middle rung sits exactly half way, since
        ! h_3 = h_2 + (1/2)^varsigma*(h_4 - h_2).
        real(8) :: housing_status_grid_expander=1.0
        real(8) :: housing_status_grid_expander_adjuster=0
        real(8) :: distance_housing_status_grid_expander
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_housing_status_grid_expander=10.d0**(-3)

        ! THE CURVATURE IS NOW AN INSTRUMENT, on the share of the housed in the
        ! MIDDLE rental. It was held at 1.8 and nothing targeted the interior
        ! of the size distribution, which is exactly what the model got wrong:
        ! 1.6 per cent of the housed in the middle rung against 24 in the data,
        ! and 94 per cent in the largest against 63. Two rungs were pinned and
        ! the one between them was left wherever the curvature happened to put
        ! it.
        !
        ! ONE MOMENT PER RUNG. h_2 is the cheapest dwelling and answers to the
        ! employed share of the homeless, because its price is what decides
        ! whether a working household can be housed at all. h_4 answers to the
        ! share at the top size. The curvature places h_3, so the share in the
        ! middle unit is its moment. This ran on the SMALLEST category's share
        ! until then, which is the same fixed point while largest_house_size
        ! holds the top share, because the three shares add to one; what was
        ! wrong was the attribution, not the answer. The smallest category's
        ! share is not h_2's moment, so nothing was left owning it.
        !
        ! THE SIGN, AND IT FLIPPED WITH THE MOMENT. h_3 = h_2 + (1/2)^varsigma
        ! (h_4 - h_2), so a LARGER varsigma pulls the middle rung down towards
        ! the smallest one, makes the middle unit cheaper, and takes mass out of
        ! the smallest category and INTO the middle one. Too little mass in the
        ! middle therefore calls for a LARGER varsigma, so the update subtracts
        ! its gap where the old one added.
        real(8), parameter :: housing_status_grid_expander_adjuster_start=1*10.d0**(-1)
        real(8), parameter :: housing_status_grid_expander_floor=10.d0**(-3)
        real(8) :: gap_housing_status_grid_expander=0
        real(8) :: previous_gap_housing_status_grid_expander=0
        integer :: have_gap_housing_status_grid_expander=0
        
        real(8) :: smallest_rental_relative_to_lowest_income=(1-perceived_probability_lower)*0.0361+perceived_probability_lower*0.19
        ! ON, and the sign is the same story as the expander. A larger smallest
        ! unit is a more expensive smallest unit, and the households that took it
        ! have nothing cheaper to move to except the street, so raising it pushes
        ! them out of the housed distribution rather than down it and the share
        ! in the smallest size FALLS. The update therefore adds the gap.
        !
        ! Deliberately smaller than the homelessness adjuster. Both this and
        ! size_homelessness move the bottom of the housing distribution, so they
        ! compete: this one sets where the affordability floor is and that one
        ! sets how bad the alternative to it is. Running them at the same speed
        ! lets them chase each other.
        real(8) :: smallest_house_size_adjuster=0.05

        ! DAMPING THE TWO HOUSING ADJUSTERS. Both were fixed steps and both
        ! oscillated: the size distribution responds sharply to the size
        ! parameters, so a step that closes the gap from one side overshoots it
        ! from the other and the loop rings instead of settling.
        !
        ! The rule is the standard one for a damped fixed point and it needs no
        ! tuning. Remember the sign of the gap. If it flips, the step jumped the
        ! root, so halve it; if it does not, the step is still walking towards
        ! the root, so let it grow back slowly. The floor stops it damping itself
        ! to a standstill on noise from the distribution iteration, the ceiling
        ! is the original step, so an undamped run is the worst this can do
        ! rather than the best.
        real(8), parameter :: house_size_adjuster_damping=0.5
        real(8), parameter :: house_size_adjuster_growth=1.2
        real(8), parameter :: house_size_adjuster_floor=10.d0**(-3)
        real(8) :: smallest_house_size_adjuster_start=0.05
        real(8) :: largest_house_size_adjuster_start=0.05
        real(8) :: previous_gap_smallest_house_size=0
        real(8) :: previous_gap_largest_house_size=0
        integer :: have_gap_smallest_house_size=0
        integer :: have_gap_largest_house_size=0
        real(8) :: gap_house_size_1
        integer :: resumed_loop_count=0
        ! Set by read_calibration_distribution. The first three loops overwrite
