        ! THE CURVATURE OF THE POSITIVE ASSET GRID, and a free setting now
        ! rather than a compile-time constant, on the author's suggestion of
        ! 1 September: "maybe you need the grid to be more concentrated at the
        ! bottom? like expander of 2 or 3?"
        !
        ! grid(i) = top * ((i-61)/24)**expander, so a bigger exponent pushes the
        ! rungs down toward zero. At the inherited 1.5 there is exactly ONE grid
        ! point between zero wealth and a year's rent on the cheapest unit --
        ! the rungs are 0.0840 and 0.2376 and the rent is 0.1200. A household
        ! deciding whether it can hold enough to stay housed is choosing between
        ! two thirds of a year's rent and two years of it, with nothing in
        ! between, which is a numerical artifact sitting exactly on the margin
        ! the model is trying to measure.
        !
        !   expander   points below a year's rent   points below average income
        !      1.5                 1                            3
        !      2.0                 2                            6
        !      2.5                 4                            8
        !      3.0                 5                            9
        !
        ! WHAT IT COSTS, and it is not nothing. The number of positive grid
        ! points is fixed at 24, so concentrating them at the bottom spreads
        ! them at the top: the last gap widens from 0.611 at 1.5 to 1.184 at
        ! 3.0. The top of the wealth distribution is where mass had already been
        ! found piling against the edge, and wealth over output and the wealth
        ! share of the bottom half are both scored, so a coarser top is a real
        ! cost paid for a finer bottom. The author has accepted it.
        ! UNSECURED BORROWING, ADDED ON THE AUTHOR'S INSTRUCTION, 2 September:
        ! "add borrowing anyway, it's a better model. Use the 5.5% interest rate."
        !
        ! The negative grid already existed for mortgage downpayments and was
        ! simply UNREACHABLE for renters: every choice loop runs from
        ! has_house*1+(1-has_house)*renter_floor_by_age(min(max(age_index,1),life_span)), and index
        ! negative_financial_grid_size is exactly zero. With
        ! include_homeownership=0 everyone is a renter, so nobody could hold a
        ! negative position -- which is why the model can produce "holds nothing"
        ! and never "owes money", and why the bottom half's share of net worth
        ! could not be pushed below about 4.8 per cent against an SCF figure of
        ! 2.5 that is net of household debt.
        !
        ! The limit is in YEARS OF AVERAGE LABOUR INCOME. 0.30 is roughly what US
        ! revolving credit supports for households that carry a balance; the
        ! author was told that a limit near 1.0 would be needed to reach 2.5 per
        ! cent on its own, which is about four times what the data bears, so this
        ! is not expected to close that gap by itself.
        integer :: include_unsecured_borrowing=0
        ! THE LOWER BOUND OF EVERY RENTER ARRAY. A renter's financial index used
        ! to start at negative_financial_grid_size -- the zero index -- because a
        ! renter could not hold a negative position, so the forty allocations
        ! keyed on is_owner gave renters no negative indices at all. Unsecured
        ! borrowing then opened twenty-five renter STATE loops down to index 1 and
        ! every one of them read and wrote past the lower bound of an array that
        ! stops at four.
        !
        ! gfortran took it silently for a long time: the borrowing-OFF control
        ! solved cleanly, passed every invariant, and returned w_bot 3.269452
        ! against a known 0.029487, homelessness 0.0114 against 0.0842, and drug
        ! use of exactly zero. The same write segfaulted on the next revision.
        ! Neither symptom names its cause, and the numbers are the more dangerous
        ! of the two because they look like an economy.
        !
        ! Set from include_unsecured_borrowing in set_renter_floor, so the OFF
        ! economy keeps the layout it always had and only the ON economy pays for
        ! the three extra points.
        ! 1.50 -> 0.20, the author: *"maybe it should be around 0.2 times average
        ! income"*. The declaration, run_one_point.sh's default and the walker's
        ! vector had drifted apart -- 1.50, 1.50 and 0.30 -- and 0.30 is what was
        ! actually solving, because the handover overrides both. All three now say
        ! 0.20 so the source cannot be read for a value the model is not running.
        real(8) :: unsecured_borrowing_limit=0.30d0
        ! THE RATE PAID ON A NEGATIVE BALANCE. The author asked first for a rate
        ! "high like on a credit card" and then for "the 5.5% interest rate" --
        ! but the 5.5 per cent they were quoting was US revolving credit as a
        ! share of annual disposable INCOME, a quantity and not a rate. It is a
        ! handover parameter so the choice costs nothing to revisit; 0.055
        ! against a risk-free 0.03 is the value they named.
        real(8) :: unsecured_borrowing_rate=0.055d0
        ! Set once per period from the limit and average labour income; the
        ! lowest grid index a renter may choose. The last period of life is
        ! forced back to zero, on the author's instruction that there is no
        ! borrowing in the last period -- nothing remains to repay it from.
        integer, dimension(life_span) :: renter_floor_by_age=0
        integer :: renter_array_floor=1
        ! ONLY THE IMPATIENT MAY BORROW. The author's instruction, given as a
        ! speed measure: the patient type discounts at 0.9998 against a gross
        ! borrowing rate of 1.055, so beta*R is above one for them and the
        ! negative grid is a region their Euler equation points away from. Barring
        ! it removes seven of the thirty-two choice points AND seven of the
        ! thirty-two state points from the patient pass, which is half the
        ! population, so the value function loop over that pass costs roughly
        ! (25/32)^2 of what it did.
        !
        ! IT IS A RESTRICTION ON THE CHOICE SET, NOT AN IDENTITY, and it is a
        ! handover parameter for that reason. A patient household young enough to
        ! face a rising income profile can want to borrow even when beta*R>1, so
        ! whether this is free has to be MEASURED at a point -- restriction off
        ! against restriction on, same container -- and not assumed. If the
        ! moments move, this is a model change and belongs with the author.
        integer :: restrict_borrowing_to_impatient=1

        real(8) :: financial_grid_expander_positive=3.0
        ! THE SAME ACCORDION ON THE NEGATIVE SIDE. The positive grid is linear in
        ! index raised to financial_grid_expander_positive, which clusters points
        ! near zero where the mass is. Debt has the same shape -- most borrowers
        ! hold a little, a few sit against the limit -- so the negative side is
        ! built the same way rather than uniformly.
        !
        ! Two rather than three: at three the deepest interior point sits at
        ! (1/7)**3 = 0.003 of the limit, which spends six of the seven points in
        ! the first half per cent of the range. Two keeps the clustering without
        ! collapsing the interior onto zero. It is handed over so it can be tuned
        ! rather than argued about.
        ! EVEN RUNGS ON THE NEGATIVE SIDE, on the author's instruction of 5
        ! September: *"0, -0.15, and -0.3"*. The accordion raises the distance
        ! from zero to this power, so at 2.0 and three points the middle rung
        ! lands at -0.075, not -0.15. At 1.0 the ladder is linear and the three
        ! points are exactly the ones asked for.
        real(8) :: financial_grid_expander_negative=1.0
                                
        integer :: largest_possible_financial_index,largest_possible_mortgage_index
        real(8) :: value_of_house_without_min_down_payment
        
        ! THE THIRD. The housing index runs lowest_housing_grid_point .. this
        ! minus one, so at 3 it is -1, 0, 1, 2: homelessness plus three rental
        ! sizes, which is the four point grid of the written model. It was 7,
        ! which gave eight statuses, and six of the extra slots were owner states
        ! that no longer exist.
        ! ONE RENTAL, on the author's instruction of 5 September: *"i suggest to
        ! just have h2"*, *"no h3, and no h4"*, *"and just choose h2 so that
        ! spending on housing is 26.8%"*.
        !
        ! WHY, in their words and in the measurement. With the housing taste
        ! pinned at its data value of 0.268 the ladder stopped doing any work:
        ! 82.3 per cent of households sat in the largest unit at ratios (1.5,
        ! 2.0) and 89.5 per cent at (2.0, 3.0), against 8.1 and 5.9 per cent in
        ! the cheapest. The author: *"it's almost like you can just have 1
        ! rental"*. It was, so the grid now says so.
        !
        ! WIDENING MADE IT WORSE, WHICH IS WHY THE THIRD RUNG IS GONE RATHER THAN
        ! RETUNED. Moving the ratios from (1.5, 2.0) to (2.0, 3.0) at a fixed
        ! bottom rung took gamma from 0.3156 to 0.3902 and the largest unit's
        ! share from 0.823 to 0.895. The prediction beforehand was the opposite --
        ! that a more expensive top rung would push households DOWN -- and it was
        ! wrong: with an honest taste, housing is a normal good and a bigger top
        ! rung is more attractive, not less.
        !
        ! The grid is now index -1 for the street and index 0 for the single
        ! rental. housing_size is dimensioned lowest_housing_grid_point to
        ! housing_status_grid_size-1, so this is (-1:0), and the rung assignment
        ! at initialize_housing_grid is already guarded by
        ! `if (housing_status_grid_size>2)` and simply does not run.
        !
        ! h_3, "share in the largest unit", is 1.0 BY CONSTRUCTION now rather than
        ! merely off target. It has been in next_batch.SKIP since 31 August on the
        ! author's *"h3 can be off"*, so nothing scored is lost, but the row is
        ! now meaningless rather than missed and should be read that way.
        ! TWO RENTALS, on the author's instruction of 5 September: *"is there a
        ! way to set h2 so that the share of spending over 50% of their income on
        ! housing is like in the data? and choose h3 so that at the housing
        ! spending is 26.8% at the chosen relative_share_of_housing of 0.268?"*
        !
        ! This supersedes the one-rental grid of an hour earlier. One rung had to
        ! set both the affordability floor and the aggregate housing share, and
        ! could not: the two moments pull on different margins. Two rungs give
        ! each its own instrument --
        !
        !   h2, the bottom rung  -> renters paying over half their INCOME on
        !                           housing, 0.175 in the SCF
        !   h3, the top rung     -> gamma, housing over expenditure, 0.268
        !
        ! with relative_share_of_housing pinned at 0.268 as data throughout.
        ! THREE RENTAL RUNGS, restored 6 September on the author's instruction.
        ! Two rungs made burden50 and h_3 both ride on h2 and the productivity
        ! grid made their requirements disjoint: burden50 = 0.175 needs node 3
        ! burdened, so h2 >= 0.1543, while h_3 = 0.627 needed h2 near 0.085.
        ! Measured over ten ladders; the table is in CALIBRATION_STATE.md.
        ! With three, h2 owns burden50 and the TOP rung owns h_3.
        ! FIVE RENTAL RUNGS, 6 September, on the author's instruction: *"add 2
        ! more rungs and then space them in some predetermined way such that the
        ! top rung gets the right share of households and the bottom rung makes
        ! it so that there are enough cost burdened renters"*.
        !
        ! THE CONCERN, RECORDED BECAUSE IT MAY BE RIGHT. h_3 counts the TOP rung
        ! only, and the middle rung of the three-rung ladder held just 7 to 16
        ! per cent of renters across the whole taste-weight bracket -- the
        ! desired-housing distribution is bimodal relative to the rungs. Two more
        ! interior rungs give households two more places to stop SHORT of the
        ! top, which mechanically pushes h_3 down.
        !
        ! THE GAIN THAT MAY OUTWEIGH IT. More rungs mean less discreteness, and
        ! discreteness is what costs gamma about 0.05: it read 0.219 at a weight
        ! of 0.268, which is why the weight had to run to 0.56 to deliver 0.268.
        ! A finer ladder should let the weight sit near its data value.
        !
        ! Which dominates is open and depends on the spacing, which is why the
        ! interior is geometric between two FREE endpoints rather than fixed
        ! ratios of the bottom.
        ! THREE RENTAL RUNGS ON FIXED RATIOS, 6 September, on the author's
        ! instruction: *"let's go with the 2x/3x rungs and concede h_3... All I
        ! want is to have the right share of cost burdened renters coming from
        ! the bottom rung, and the relative share of housing to be high and match
        ! the housing spending share"*.
        !
        ! ONE FREE PARAMETER SETS THE WHOLE RENTAL LADDER: h2 owns burden50 and
        ! the upper rungs follow at 2h2 and 3h2. h_3 therefore has NO instrument
        ! and is CONCEDED -- see SKIP in next_batch.py. largest_house_size
        ! determines nothing here and must be held rather than calibrated;
        ! leaving it mapped to h_3 would be an instrument that cannot move its
        ! target, which is how the moment ran away before.
        !
        ! WHAT h_3 COSTS, measured at h2 = 0.20 on the free-top-rung ladder: the
        ! share in the largest unit ran 0.1613, 0.1242, 0.0944, 0.0441 at top
        ! rungs of 1.30x, 1.50x, 1.80x and 2.25x h2, a log-log slope of -2.32.
        ! Extrapolated to 3x that is about 0.025 against a target of 0.627.
        integer, parameter :: housing_status_grid_size=3
                         
