        ! THE ASSET LOTTERY, carried from the a' search to internal_computations.
        ! Threadprivate for the same reason the vectors it is built from are.
        real(8) :: interp_frac_1=0
        integer :: interp_neighbour_1=0
        ! THE ASSET LOTTERY BREAKS THE HOUSEHOLD BUDGET CONSTRAINT, so it is off.
        !
        ! compute_asset_lottery locates the true optimum between two grid points
        ! by fitting a parabola, then sets the weight so that the lottery's MEAN
        ! asset holding equals that vertex. The mean is therefore moved off the
        ! grid point, which is the whole purpose. But consumption is computed
        ! once, in internal_computations, as
        !
        !     c = (resources - a(fin_index_1)) / (1+tau_c)
        !
        ! from the GRID POINT and never from the vertex. So the household is
        ! recorded consuming as though it saved a(fin_index_1) while saving
        ! frac*a(neighbour) + (1-frac)*a(fin_index_1) in expectation, and its
        ! budget fails by exactly
        !
        !     frac * (a(neighbour) - a(fin_index_1)).
        !
        ! MEASURED, NOT ARGUED. Instrumenting check_budget_constraint_clears to
        ! print the weight, both grid points, consumption and resources, 105 of
        ! the violations matched that expression to eleven decimal places, and
        ! every record whose weight was zero cleared exactly. The violations run
        ! to eight per cent of consumption, and every one of them is a homeless
        ! household, because homelessness is where the savings policy sits
        ! between grid points often enough for the parabola to fire.
        !
        ! Turning it off restores the identity exactly, at the cost of the grid
        ! rounding in the savings policy that it was written to remove. That is
        ! the right trade: a model whose households cannot pay for what they
        ! consume is worse than one whose savings sit on a grid.
        !
        ! TO PUT IT BACK, consumption has to be computed from the lottery's mean
        ! rather than from the grid point, which means the lottery has to sit
        ! INSIDE the optimisation rather than be applied after it: the flow
        ! utility changes with consumption, so the argmax can move. That is a
        ! real change to the a' search and not a one line fix.
        ! OFF, AND THE REASON IS NOT THE ONE IN SECTIONS 11 AND 15. Those
        ! describe two defects in the lottery, and section 11's is now fixed: it
        ! reads the winning branch's own three points, and with that its refusal
        ! rate on the branch match test went from 90.9 per cent to zero and it
        ! fired 159,822 times in 177,120 rather than twelve.
        !
        ! IT STILL SHOULD NOT BE ON, because the model already does what the
        ! lottery was for. internal_computations refines a' on a sub grid of
        ! fin_num_of_internal = 50 points spanning the interval around the coarse
        ! point, so the policy's a' is already off grid and already fine. The
        ! lottery then places a SECOND, coarser estimate of the same quantity,
        ! fitted to a parabola through three COARSE points, and the distribution
        ! carries that while the policy carries the refined one. They disagree,
        ! and the disagreement is the budget residual section 15 measured: with
        ! the lottery on and section 11 fixed the check still fired 31,530 times.
        !
        ! So the coarse grid is a SEARCH grid and the refinement is what sets
        ! precision. That is the ground on which a coarser grid should be argued,
        ! not the lottery.
        integer, parameter :: interpolate_a_prime=0
        !$omp threadprivate(interp_frac_1)
        !$omp threadprivate(interp_neighbour_1)
        ! THE LOTTERY'S OWN DIAGNOSTICS, and they are NOT threadprivate.
        !
        ! A threadprivate counter is unreadable after the fact: the copies of
        ! every thread but the master are discarded when the parallel region
        ! ends, so a counter that lives where the counting happens cannot be
        ! printed where the reporting happens. That is why the mismatch count
        ! sat in the code incremented and never read.
        !
        ! Shared with an atomic is the other wrong answer. The subroutine runs
        ! once per state per housing branch per age, which is tens of millions
        ! of calls, and an atomic on a single word would serialise the search.
        !
        ! So each thread writes its OWN SLOT of a shared array, which needs no
        ! synchronisation because no two threads touch the same element, and
        ! the row stride pushes the slots onto separate cache lines so the
        ! threads are not fighting over one line either. The totals are summed
        ! in serial code afterwards.
        !   row 1  calls with the lottery switched on
        !   row 2  refusals because the vectors held the wrong housing branch
        !   row 3  refusals for no bracket, no concavity, or a vertex outside it
        !   row 4  calls that actually returned a positive fraction
        !
        ! The slot index is resolved on FIRST USE by each thread rather than
        ! set once in a parallel region of its own. Threadprivate copies are
        ! only guaranteed to persist between two parallel regions when the
        ! thread count matches and dynamic adjustment is off, and a slot that
        ! silently failed to persist would put two threads on one element and
        ! lose counts in a race. Zero means unresolved, so a thread that gets a
        ! fresh copy resolves it again.
        integer, parameter :: interp_counter_stride=8
        integer(8) :: interp_counters(interp_counter_stride,max_CPUs)=0
        integer :: interp_slot_1=0
        !$omp threadprivate(interp_slot_1)
        ! One line per equilibrium solve, so leave it on: it is the record of
        ! whether the coarse grid is defensible in the run that produced the
        ! numbers, rather than in some earlier run someone remembers.
        integer, parameter :: report_lottery_diagnostics=1

